    {
      "id": "grammar-pasarle-algo-a-alguien",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "pasarle algo a alguien",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that something happens to a particular person?",
      "answerEn": "Name what happens, and put me, te, le, nos, os, or les before a changed form of pasar to identify the affected person. You may also name that person after the Spanish word a.",
      "noteEn": "Pasar changes to match the thing or event that happens, not the affected person: esto me pasa but estas cosas me pasan. With the verb's unchanged dictionary form, attach the short pronoun to the end: pasarle. In the prompt, algo stands for what happens and alguien for the affected person; those words do not have to appear literally. Spanish may repeat the affected person after a, a word often translated as ‘to,’ for clarity or emphasis, while English normally states the person once.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Eso me pasó a mí ayer.",
        "en": "That happened to me yesterday."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A2",
        "grammar",
        "object pronouns"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
      "schedulable": true,
